WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
Secure C++ libraries start with the public API.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:07.480
Treat every public function as part of a consumer's security model.

3
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:16.700
Keep interfaces narrow, use clear names, prefer safe defaults, use strong types for important choices, and make sensitive operations explicit enough to review.

4
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.360
The API should guide callers toward correct use.

5
00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.720
Make contracts visible.

6
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:29.660
Document ownership, lifetime, cleanup, error behavior, thread safety, compatibility, and configuration.

7
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:38.560
Avoid hidden global state, unclear allocator boundaries, and ambiguous behavior that forces callers to guess how the library should be used.

8
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:42.740
Clear contracts reduce support burden and make wrapper code safer.

9
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.700
Validate before trust.

10
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.880
Check sizes, ranges, encodings, structure, and resource limits at boundaries.

11
00:00:50.980 --> 00:01:03.060
Preserve invariants on failure, return useful errors, and test malformed input, likely misuse, concurrency behavior, and compatibility across supported platforms and build modes.

12
00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:06.780
Finally, maintain the library as a product.

13
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:17.920
Minimize and track dependencies, keep build options reviewable, publish clear release notes, support vulnerability response, and give consumers a path to update safely.

14
00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:22.820
A secure library helps every consuming application make safer decisions.

15
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:29.320
Its design quality travels wherever the library is reused, so governance is part of the API promise.

16
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:36.460
Secure habits compound across every downstream integration over time and responsible maintenance.
